endobj

613 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 273 0 R/E(The Security Council\220s institutional design emerged from the failure of the League of Nations system to prevent major-power conflict and the experience of the Second World War. The UN Charter therefore gave the Security Council primary responsibility for international peace and security and created a structure in which the major powers would participate directly in collective-security decisions. The permanent-member veto was a political compromise intended to prevent the organization from taking enforcement action against a major power without that power\220s concurrence.)/Pg 18 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

