641 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 274 0 R/T(1. Mandate of the United Nations Security Council)/E(1. Mandate of the United Nations Security Council)/Pg 19 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

642 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(The United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime \(UNODC\) is a UN body dedicated to combating illicit drugs, transnational organized crime, corruption, terrorism, and other emerging security threats. Through international cooperation, policy development, research, and technical assistance, UNODC supports Member States in strengthening justice systems, preventing crime, and protecting communities. As criminal networks increasingly operate across borders and exploit new technologies, the committee provides a platform for delegates to develop effective, cooperative, and sustainable solutions to these complex global challenges.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

643 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 274 0 R/T(Purpose and composition)/E(Purpose and composition)/Pg 19 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

644 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 274 0 R/Pg 19 0 R/A[1590 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

645 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(mun@umt.edu.pk)/Pg 19 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

646 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(umt_mun)/Pg 19 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

647 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(This history explains a recurring tension in Council practice: the Council possesses unusually strong formal tools, but their use depends on political agreement among States with very different strategic interests. The result is that UNSC effectiveness cannot be assessed solely by the wording of a resolution. Implementation, political will, cooperation from States and the Council\220s ability to maintain unity are equally important.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

648 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(The Security Council was designed after the Second World War to provide a stronger collective-security mechanism than the League of Nations had achieved. The Charter combines sovereign equality with differentiated responsibility for the five permanent members.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

649 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(The historical practice matters because the boundaries are not merely theoretical. The Council has used Chapter VII for sanctions regimes, tribunals, arms restrictions and other measures, while also invoking Chapter VII in connection with peace operations. Delegates should therefore distinguish the legal basis of a measure from the political purpose for which it is adopted.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

650 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(When the Council determines a threat to the peace, breach of the peace or act of aggression, Chapter VII provides stronger tools. Article 41 allows measures not involving armed force, including economic and diplomatic restrictions. Article 42 concerns action involving armed force when Article 41 measures are inadequate.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

651 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(Regional organizations can play roles in peace and security, but their activities interact with Security Council authority. The relationship is especially important for mediation, peace operations, sanctions implementation and regional stabilization.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

652 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 274 0 R/E(The Council's practice has changed markedly since the Cold War, and delegates should be able to speak to that trajectory. The veto has been cast more than 300 times since the Council's first resolution in January 1946, but its pattern has shifted substantially: the Soviet Union cast the overwhelming majority of vetoes in the Council's first decade, largely to block new UN membership applications, while the United States has used the veto most frequently since the 1970s, predominantly on resolutions concerning Israel and Palestine. A brief window of intensive P5 cooperation followed the end of the Cold War, most visibly in the Council's unified response authorizing action after Iraq's 1990 invasion of Kuwait, and several multi-year stretches through the 1990s passed without any veto at all.)/Pg 19 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

