endobj

850 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(umt_mun)/Pg 24 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

851 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(Sanctions are among the Council\220s most frequently used non-military tools. They can include asset freezes, travel bans, arms embargoes, commodity restrictions and other targeted measures. The Council often creates sanctions committees and expert panels to support implementation and monitoring.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

852 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 279 0 R/T(1.5. Core Principles)/E(1.5. Core Principles)/Pg 24 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

853 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(Every operative clause should be traceable to a Security Council competence and an identifiable legal or political basis. Delegates should distinguish recommendations from binding decisions and avoid assigning powers to the Council that belong to other UN organs or Member States.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

854 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 279 0 R/T(Sovereignty and peaceful settlement)/E(Sovereignty and peaceful settlement)/Pg 24 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

855 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(Council action should respect sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence while recognizing that serious threats to international peace and security may require collective action under the Charter.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

856 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 279 0 R/T(Charter legality and mandate fit)/E(Charter legality and mandate fit)/Pg 24 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

857 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 279 0 R/T(Necessity, proportionality and civilian protection)/E(Necessity, proportionality and civilian protection)/Pg 24 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

858 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(Security measures should be calibrated to the threat and designed to minimize foreseeable harm to civilians and civilian infrastructure. \215Protection\216 should be translated into concrete tasks, resources and monitoring.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

859 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 279 0 R/T(Consent, impartiality and operational clarity in peacekeeping)/E(Consent, impartiality and operational clarity in peacekeeping)/Pg 24 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

860 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 279 0 R/E(Peacekeeping mandates should be matched to available capabilities and political conditions. Delegates should not assume the UN possesses a standing army; Member States provide personnel, equipment and financing.)/Pg 24 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

