889 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 280 0 R/E(The United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime \(UNODC\) is a UN body dedicated to combating illicit drugs, transnational organized crime, corruption, terrorism, and other emerging security threats. Through international cooperation, policy development, research, and technical assistance, UNODC supports Member States in strengthening justice systems, preventing crime, and protecting communities. As criminal networks increasingly operate across borders and exploit new technologies, the committee provides a platform for delegates to develop effective, cooperative, and sustainable solutions to these complex global challenges.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

890 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 280 0 R/T(Purpose and composition)/E(Purpose and composition)/Pg 25 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

891 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 280 0 R/Pg 25 0 R/A[1590 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

892 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 280 0 R/E(mun@umt.edu.pk)/Pg 25 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

893 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 280 0 R/E(umt_mun)/Pg 25 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

894 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 280 0 R/E(Council measures should be based on credible evidence, clear listing or designation criteria where sanctions are used, reporting arrangements, and review mechanisms that permit correction of error.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

895 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 280 0 R/T(Regional ownership and coordination)/E(Regional ownership and coordination)/Pg 25 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

896 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 280 0 R/T(Accountability and evidence)/E(Accountability and evidence)/Pg 25 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

897 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 280 0 R/E(Regional actors can provide proximity, mediation channels and political leverage. Council resolutions should define how regional and UN efforts coordinate rather than creating parallel or contradictory mechanisms.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

898 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 280 0 R/T(Women, peace and security)/E(Women, peace and security)/Pg 25 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

899 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 280 0 R/E(Peace processes are stronger when women participate meaningfully in prevention, mediation, negotiation and implementation, while conflict-related risks to women and girls are explicitly addressed.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

900 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 280 0 R/T(Review, adaptability and exit)/E(Review, adaptability and exit)/Pg 25 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

901 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 280 0 R/E(Every major mechanism should have a reporting cycle, review point, measurable indicators and conditions for modification, transition or termination.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

902 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 280 0 R/T(1.6. Contemporary Case Studies and Violations)/E(1.6. Contemporary Case Studies and Violations)/Pg 25 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

903 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 280 0 R/T(MONUSCO)/E(MONUSCO)/Pg 25 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

904 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 280 0 R/E(The UN mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo illustrates the complexity of a long-running operation with civilian-protection and stabilization tasks. The Council must balance mandate ambition with host-state consent, armed-group dynamics, regional politics, mission capacity and eventual transition.)/Pg 25 0 R/K[19]>>
endobj

