979 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 282 0 R/E(Sudan-related missions demonstrate how mandates evolve as political conditions change. The Council must think about transition, successor mechanisms and what happens after a mission closes. \215Exit strategy\216 should not mean simply leaving; it should identify which national, regional or UN mechanisms inherit residual responsibilities.)/Pg 27 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

980 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 282 0 R/T(Ukraine \204 veto politics and Council paralysis)/E(Ukraine \204 veto politics and Council paralysis)/Pg 27 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

981 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 282 0 R/E(The Ukraine case demonstrates the structural significance of the veto. Russia, as a permanent member, can block substantive Council decisions that it opposes, even where other members support stronger Council action. This does not mean the UN system becomes completely inactive: the General Assembly, Secretary-General, humanitarian agencies, courts and other institutions can continue to act within their respective mandates. But it does illustrate the limits of the Security Council when a permanent member is directly involved in a conflict or strongly contests the proposed response. Historically, this reflects the basic political bargain embedded in the Charter. The veto was designed to ensure that enforcement action by the organization would not proceed against the wishes of a major power. That bargain protects the Council\220s participation of the major powers while simultaneously creating the possibility of paralysis.)/Pg 27 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

982 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 282 0 R/E(The 2011 Libya case is frequently used to illustrate the difference between authorization and implementation. Resolution 1973 authorized Member States to take \215all necessary measures\216 to protect civilians and established a no-fly zone, while excluding an occupying foreign force. The intervention contributed to the collapse of Muammar Gaddafi\220s government, but Libya subsequently experienced prolonged political fragmentation and insecurity.)/Pg 27 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

983 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 282 0 R/T(Libya \204 authorization and unintended consequences)/E(Libya \204 authorization and unintended consequences)/Pg 27 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

984 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 282 0 R/T(UNMIS / UNAMID / Sudan experience)/E(UNMIS / UNAMID / Sudan experience)/Pg 27 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

