1018 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 283 0 R/E(The 2026 United States\205Iran conflict is a particularly relevant contemporary case for an open-agenda Security Council because it combines questions of the use of force, self-defence, regional spillover, civilian protection, maritime security, sanctions, nuclear verification, mediation and Council unity. The United States reported to the Security Council that it commenced combat operations against Iran on 28 February 2026 pursuant to Article 51 and its claimed inherent right of self-defence. Iran has disputed the legality of the U.S. and Israeli actions and invoked its own Article 51 position.)/Pg 28 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

1019 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 283 0 R/E(The conflict quickly acquired a wider regional dimension. Iranian missile and drone attacks affected Bahrain, Kuwait, Oman, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates and Jordan. On 11 March 2026, the Security Council adopted Resolution 2817 by a vote of 13 in favour, with China and the Russian Federation abstaining. The resolution condemned Iranian attacks, demanded their cessation, reaffirmed navigational rights and condemned attempts to obstruct international navigation through the Strait of Hormuz.Diplomacy produced a significant but fragile development in June. The Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding between the United States and Iran created a framework for de-escalation, while Pakistan and Qatar facilitated follow-up talks. The subsequent roadmap included technical discussions on nuclear issues, sanctions, monitoring and dispute resolution, as well as communication and deconfliction arrangements intended to reduce incidents and support safe commercial passage through the Strait of Hormuz.)/Pg 28 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

1020 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 283 0 R/E(By early September 2026, however, the conflict had escalated again. Reuters reported renewed U.S. strikes on Iran beginning 1 September, followed by Iranian attacks on U.S.-linked positions and regional targets. On 5 September, U.S. and Iranian forces exchanged fire near Iranian waters, and the United States struck t)/Pg 28 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

1021 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 283 0 R/E(Ukraine demonstrates a central institutional problem: when a permanent member is directly implicated in a conflict, the Council may struggle to adopt substantive measures because of veto dynamics. Delegates should distinguish what the Council can do collectively from what other UN organs, regional organizations or states can do separately.)/Pg 28 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

1022 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 283 0 R/T(Case Study: 2026 United States\205Iran Conflict)/E(Case Study: 2026 United States\205Iran Conflict)/Pg 28 0 R/K[13]>>
