1090 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/P 285 0 R/T(1. Mandate of the United Nations Security Council)/E(1. Mandate of the United Nations Security Council)/Pg 30 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1091 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 285 0 R/E(The United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime \(UNODC\) is a UN body dedicated to combating illicit drugs, transnational organized crime, corruption, terrorism, and other emerging security threats. Through international cooperation, policy development, research, and technical assistance, UNODC supports Member States in strengthening justice systems, preventing crime, and protecting communities. As criminal networks increasingly operate across borders and exploit new technologies, the committee provides a platform for delegates to develop effective, cooperative, and sustainable solutions to these complex global challenges.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1092 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 285 0 R/T(Purpose and composition)/E(Purpose and composition)/Pg 30 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1093 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/Figure/P 285 0 R/Pg 30 0 R/A[1590 0 R 0]/K[6]>>
endobj

1094 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 285 0 R/E(mun@umt.edu.pk)/Pg 30 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

1095 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 285 0 R/E(umt_mun)/Pg 30 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

1096 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 285 0 R/T(Sanctions evasion and unintended humanitarian effects)/E(Sanctions evasion and unintended humanitarian effects)/Pg 30 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

1097 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 285 0 R/E(Sanctions may be circumvented through illicit networks while legitimate banks, insurers and humanitarian organizations may over-comply because of legal or reputational risk. Resolution 2664\220s standing humanitarian exemption illustrates the Council\220s attempt to reduce such effects, but implementation remains complex.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

1098 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 285 0 R/T(Regional coordination problems)/E(Regional coordination problems)/Pg 30 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

1099 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 285 0 R/E(Multiple mediators or regional actors can produce duplication, contradictory incentives or competing political tracks. Council resolutions should define communication channels, reporting arrangements and division of labour.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

1100 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 285 0 R/T(Verification and accountability)/E(Verification and accountability)/Pg 30 0 R/K[13]>>
endobj

1101 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 285 0 R/E(A ceasefire, sanctions regime or political agreement cannot be reliably enforced without credible information. The Council must identify who verifies compliance, how information is reported and what happens when evidence is disputed.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[14]>>
endobj

1102 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 285 0 R/T(Emerging technologies and changing warfare)/E(Emerging technologies and changing warfare)/Pg 30 0 R/K[15]>>
endobj

1103 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 285 0 R/E(Drones, cyber operations, autonomous systems, commercial satellite imagery and information operations can alter conflict dynamics faster than Council procedures adapt. Resolutions should focus on durable principles and verifiable conduct rather than narrow technology labels.)/Pg 30 0 R/K[16]>>
endobj

1104 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/P 285 0 R/T(The humanitarian dilemma)/E(The humanitarian dilemma)/Pg 30 0 R/K[17]>>
endobj

1105 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 285 0 R/E(Sanctions and security measures can pressure armed actors but may also affect civilians if poorly designed. Banks, insurers and humanitarian organizations frequently respond to legal uncertainty by over-complying: declining to process any transaction connected to a sanctioned country, entity or region rather than assessing individual risk. This is the same de-risking dynamic that affects FIUs )/Pg 30 0 R/K[18]>>
endobj

